Date: Fri, 26 Feb 93 14:27:56 PST
From: memexis!tribble@uunet.UU.NET (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: POLI: In Praise of Martial Arts Training

	 From: uunet!snark.thyrsus.com!esr (Eric S. Raymond)

I agree with many of Eric Raymond's (I too study martial arts).  Since
I love that whole area of discussion, I want to respond to a little
bit of it.

Martial arts is an excellent defense against rape/mugging/etc. *not*
because you can handle your attackers, but because your body posture
reduces the probability of attack.  People tend not to mess with
someone who holds themselves like they know how to take care of
themselves.  Good training in anything from guns to 'martial arts' can
give that attitude; I suspect it largely comes from thinking about the
issues and knowing how you will respond.

	 Until you *know*, in your gut, what you would do when faced with the threat
	 of lethal aggression, all your intellectual choices about violence will be
	 sham, piffle, purest bullshit.

Unfortunately I think you can't know in your gut except in retrospect:
when you have fought someone, then you know you won't freeze up.  I
would also note that you can make intellectual decisions about what
result you would like, then hire someone (a PPL) to provide that
result for you because you know you aren't capable physically or
emotionally of doing so.  I might think it appropriate that rapists be
gelded, but I wouldn't want to do it myself.

	 There are two issues here.  One is learning truly effective self-defense.  For
	 that, the right thing is to buy a handgun and learn to maintain and use it.

Then do you carry it?  BTW I recommend indoor paintball as a further
exercise.  It is truly educational and terrifying how easily you get
killed.

	 The other issue is developing what I think of as "warrior mind" --- a gut-
	 psychologically disconnects you from the violence.  You don't feel your
	 bullet striking.

I don't think this is true.  The immediacy of the effect is within the
timing window that our reflexes quickly relate to cause and effect.
One of the reasons shooting can be so exhilerating is that your brain
wiring understands that it caused something to happen *at a distance*.

	 This is where martial arts comes in.  It's much less likely to save your
	 your torso.  Violence is personal, real, immediate.  You learn to make use-
	 of-force decisions quickly and correctly even while under physical stress.

Hmmm.  Violence doesn't have to be personal.  It's important to learn
just how easy destruction is, and that takes a long time to learn when
you're just doing hand-to-hand.  Also, it's easy to fool yourself in
martial arts that you can use less than lethal force.  In a real
fight, you have to be willing to kill, and then make occasional
bexceptions.  (This is not a problem for minor situations like bullies
and such.)  Further, in an anarchic world, especially with advanced
technology, it's even more important to understand and be able to
handle less immediate cause and effect is defense.  In some sense,
immediacy teaches tactics, but indirect violence teaches strategy.

	 that is extremely valuable for *avoiding* violence.  Only the man (or woman)
	 with warrior mind can be truly peaceful --- the untrained, under pressure, are

Well I certainly wouldn't say only :-)

	 is much less important than quality of instruction --- all styles merge
	 together at the higher levels.  Find a good school and do it!

Quality of instruction is definitely the most important dimension.  A
lot of schools go through the motionis but don't teach the
fundamentals well. 

Other things to consider: Styles put different amounts of
stress on the body (karates are more stressful than Tai Chi Chuan, for
instance, but also easier to use effectively).  Styles have different
techniques for attack and defense.  Finally, schools manage the
balance between training for competition and sparring with training
for combat.  These two attitudes are quite different (the latter is
harder to find), and not complementary.  When trained for combat,
sparring can be *extremely* dangerous, but not enough sparring means
that you don't know how you'll do.  Likewise, training for competition
means that your body absorbs a bogus set of rules of etiquette in
fighting that have no place in a real situation.  This problem changes
as you get better.

The obviouos solution is learn several different styles :-)

