Date: Sat, 27 Feb 93 08:11:39 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Mike offers the bet where the bettor's money halves 60% of the time and
doubles 40% of the time.

As Dale Worley basically showed, this is a positive-return bet.  The more
money you bet, and the more times you bet it, the more money you will
have at the end, on the average.  So I would certainly take up Mike's
offer.

Steve suggests that over a period of time the amount of money will gradually
decrease on the average.  This is what I thought, too, at first, but Dale
showed that this is wrong.  The reason is that, although in log terms the
average sum seems to decrease, there is also a spreading of the range of
possible outcomes.  The mean goes down but the standard deviation goes up.
Log analysis underestimates the impact of a low-probability but high-return
outcome, relative to linear analysis.

If you work out all the possible outcomes after any number of bets, you
will find that your expected return is about 10% per "round" of this bet.

