Date: Sat, 27 Feb 93 12:20:32 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

> Hal writes:

  >> My problem was, I came up with a bet with a negative log return but
  >> a positive linear return: a 60% chance that your money would halve,
  >> and a 40% chance that your money would double.  It seems like someone
  >> who believes that linear return is better would take this bet, and
  >> someone who believes that log return is better would offer it.  But
  >> would anyone actually offer it?

> I would.  Any takers?

> Mike Price (price@price.demon.co.uk)

I would under the following provisos: The duration of each bet must be
negligible in investment terms, say one week or less. And it would be rash to
have a large sum staked on a single bet. Also I believe in level stakes rather
than following staking plans like lowering the stake after a loss and raising
after a win. (If a system makes a level-stake loss, it is worthless.)

I'm staking one half unit; if I win my gain is twice this stake. I have a
40% chance of winning. After a large number of bets I would expect to have
lost 0.6 x t and gained 0.4 x 2 x t, where t is the total turnover
staked. So I gain by 0.2t .

The other way to calculate this is by the formula I gave in a previous post.
Add one to the odds obtained (which are 2 / 1 against), multiply this by 40
(win percentage), subtract 100 and this gives the percentage return on
turnover (20%). 

What if Mike says that this is against the terms and conditions of the bet?
Say the bettor has to stake a very large sum, and just keep it invested with
the bookie. If it halves, this amount then doubles or halves, and so on. So
on average, the total does a random walk around the initial sum but gradually
drifts down since the log return is negative.

It depends on whether the investors are allowed to pull out at any time. I'd
set up a syndicate of, say, 100 investors. They agree to pool their individual
wins and losses together, so that a negative log return is converted into a
positive linear return. After, say, fifty bets, the members withdraw and split
the profits between them.

The syndicate is voluntary, by the way, so I don't think this is proof of
the validity of socialism.   ;-)  

For level stakes, linear return is certainly best. When the layer controls the 
total bankroll, log return has to be considered, as a negative log return
would bring the total down, longterm. But gains from the winning investments
would still outstrip losses from losers, for a positive linear return, given
that the number of bets was not extremely high. After an extremely long series
the layer would be guaranteed a return when the log return was negative.
But this is obtained by denying the bettor the right to bet in level stakes.

If we return to the original idea of stock investments, the broker does not
force the investor to invest according to the result of his previous
investment, neither is there a requirement that the investor must put 100%
of his money into one stock rather than splitting 1% in each of 100 stocks.

